Problem Detail Error Responses

  A function reports an error by throwing.  An escalation handler turns the exception into an HTTP
  response.  This tutorial gives those responses a consistent, structured shape:
  {{{https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7807}RFC 7807 Problem Detail}}, served as
  <<<application/problem+json>>>.

  Three handlers cover three kinds of error.  A domain exception becomes a 404.  A framework validation
  failure becomes a 400 with a field-by-field breakdown.  Anything else becomes a 500 that does not leak
  internal detail.  Registering them at office level means every endpoint gets the same error contract
  with no per-endpoint wiring.

  {{{https://github.com/officefloor/OfficeFloor/tree/master/tutorials/springboot/SpringRestProblemDetailHttpServer}Tutorial Source}}


*Problem Detail

  Spring's {{{https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/http/ProblemDetail.html}ProblemDetail}} is the RFC 7807 model.
  It carries <<<type>>>, <<<title>>>, <<<status>>>, <<<detail>>>, and <<<instance>>>, and it allows extra
  properties.  A handler builds one and sends it in a <<<ResponseEntity>>>.  OfficeFloor serialises it as
  <<<application/problem+json>>>.


*A domain exception: 404

  The read endpoint throws <<<NotFoundException>>> when the article is absent.  The endpoints here are kept
  as single functions so the focus stays on the responses.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestproblemdetail/GetArticle.java}

  A handler registered under <<<officefloor/escalation/>>> maps the exception to a 404 Problem Detail:

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/net.officefloor.tutorial.springrestproblemdetail.NotFoundException.yml}

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestproblemdetail/NotFoundExceptionHandler.java}

  <<<GET /article/99999>>> then returns:

+--
{
  "type": "https://example.com/problems/not-found",
  "title": "Article not found",
  "status": 404,
  "detail": "No article exists with id 99999",
  "timestamp": "2026-07-09T12:00:00Z"
}
+--


*A validation failure: 400 with field errors

  The create endpoint validates the body with <<<@Valid>>>.  A blank title makes the framework throw
  <<<MethodArgumentNotValidException>>>.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestproblemdetail/CreateArticle.java}

  Its handler maps to a 400 and adds a field-by-field breakdown, so a client can show which fields failed:

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.web.bind.MethodArgumentNotValidException.yml}

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestproblemdetail/ValidationExceptionHandler.java}

  <<<POST /article>>> with a blank title returns:

+--
{
  "type": "https://example.com/problems/validation",
  "title": "Request validation failed",
  "status": 400,
  "detail": "The request has 1 invalid field(s)",
  "timestamp": "2026-07-09T12:00:00Z",
  "errors": { "title": "title is required" }
}
+--


*Anything else: 500 without leaking

  A handler for <<<Exception>>> is the catch-all.  It maps any exception with no more specific handler to a
  500, with a generic message so internal detail never reaches the client.

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestproblemdetail/Boom.java}

%{snippet|file=${basedir}/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/java.lang.Exception.yml}

%{snippet|id=tutorial|file=${basedir}/src/main/java/net/officefloor/tutorial/springrestproblemdetail/GeneralExceptionHandler.java}

  <<<GET /boom>>> throws an <<<IllegalStateException>>> with an internal message, but the client only sees:

+--
{
  "type": "about:blank",
  "title": "Internal server error",
  "status": 500,
  "detail": "An unexpected error occurred while processing the request",
  "timestamp": "2026-07-09T12:00:00Z"
}
+--


*Most specific wins

  All three handlers are active at once.  Escalation is most specific first.  <<<NotFoundException>>> and
  <<<MethodArgumentNotValidException>>> each have their own handler, so they are used ahead of the
  <<<Exception>>> catch-all.  The catch-all only runs for exceptions nothing else claims.  See the
  {{{../SpringRestExceptionHttpServer/index.html}Exception}} tutorial for escalation routing in depth.


*Testing

  The test drives all three error responses and checks the status, the <<<application/problem+json>>>
  content type, and the body fields.

